\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{A constant flux on a torus}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{Let #{F=\mathbb C}, let #{w} be an integer with #{|w|<N_1N_2/2}, and put
#{\varphi=2\pi w/(N_1N_2)}. With coordinates #{0\le x_j<N_j}, let
#{U_2(x)=\ue^{\ui\varphi x_1}}, let #{U_1(x)=\ue^{-2\pi\ui wx_2/N_2}} when #{x_1=N_1-1} and
#{U_1(x)=1} otherwise, and let #{U_j(x)=1} for #{j\ge 3}. Then #{u_p=\ue^{\ui\varphi}} for
every plaquette #{p=(x;1,2)}. An argument of #{u_p} is the sum of the displayed phases of
#{U_1(x)} and #{U_2(x+e_1)} minus those of #{U_1(x+e_2)} and #{U_2(x)}. When
#{x_1<N_1-1}, the links in direction #{1} are trivial and the two links in direction #{2}
differ in phase by #{\varphi}. On the last row #{x_1=N_1-1}, the links in direction #{2}
contribute #{-(N_1-1)\varphi}, because #{x_1+1} wraps to #{0}, and the two links in
direction #{1} contribute #{2\pi w/N_2}, with an extra #{-2\pi w} when #{x_2=N_2-1},
because #{x_2+1} wraps to #{0}. Together these give #{\varphi} at every site of this
plane except the corner #{x_1=N_1-1}, #{x_2=N_2-1}, where they give #{\varphi -2\pi w}.
Both values give the same unit complex number, whose principal argument is #{\varphi}.
Every plaquette in another plane has #{u_p=1}. All
plaquettes are plain, since #{|\varphi|<\pi}. For fixed #{x_3,\ldots,x_n} the sum #{T} of the
#{N_1N_2} plaquettes #{(x;1,2)} is a [closed plaquette surface](fdip-015V), because its
links cancel in pairs by periodicity, and #{\operatorname{fl}(T)=\frac{N_1N_2\varphi}{2\pi}=w}.}
